The '''Energies Renovables de l'Estat de Balboa''' (EREB) is a [[state-owned enterprise]] responsible for renewable energy generation in [[Balboa]], excluding hydropower. The company operates offshore wind farms, solar installations, bagasse-fired biomass plants linked to the sugar industry, and a waste-to-energy facility in [[Sant Cristòfor]]. Combined, EREB's sources account for approximately 15 per cent of Balboa's electricity supply.

EREB was established in 2002 under the framework of the [[1996 Adonian Community Edict on a Green Transition for the New Millennium]], which provided concessional financing for AC member states to develop low-carbon power generation.

== History ==

=== Establishment (2002) ===

EREB was created in 2002 to consolidate and expand Balboa's non-hydro renewable energy sector. The company was established under the Ministry of Energy and Natural Resources, with initial funding from the AC International Cooperation for Renewable Energy programme.

Prior to EREB's establishment, renewable energy in Balboa was limited to small-scale bagasse-fired plants operated by agricultural cooperatives in the sugar-producing provinces. EREB absorbed these operations and began developing new capacity in offshore wind, solar, and waste-to-energy.

=== Development ===

EREB's early focus was on bagasse/biomass, leveraging Balboa's established sugar industry in [[Jala Province|Jala]] and [[Migjorn Province|Migjorn]]. The company partnered with agricultural cooperatives to upgrade existing bagasse-fired plants and build new ones, providing a modest but stable source of renewable electricity.

In the 2010s, EREB began developing offshore wind farms along the Shendan and Aethiopian coasts. Onshore wind was considered but faced opposition from environmental groups and [[Birú people|Birú]] communities, similar to the opposition that had blocked hydroelectric expansion in the Yalabirga highlands. Offshore wind avoided land use conflicts but was more expensive to construct and maintain.

Solar capacity was limited by Balboa's wet tropical climate, which reduces the capacity factor of solar installations. EREB operates a small number of utility-scale solar farms in the drier coastal areas, as well as rooftop solar installations on government buildings in Sant Cristòfor.

A waste-to-energy plant was commissioned in Sant Cristòfor in 2016, processing municipal waste from the metropolitan area and providing a small but consistent source of electricity.

== Operations ==

* '''Headquarters''': [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Balboa]]
* '''Offshore wind share''': approximately 4% of Balboa's electricity (2026)
* '''Solar share''': approximately 3% of Balboa's electricity (2026)
* '''Bagasse/biomass share''': approximately 6% of Balboa's electricity (2026)
* '''Waste-to-energy share''': approximately 2% of Balboa's electricity (2026)
* '''Employees''': approximately 1,100 (2026)

EREB operates a distributed portfolio of renewable energy assets across Balboa:

* '''Offshore wind''': Wind farms along the Shendan and Aethiopian coasts
* '''Solar''': Utility-scale solar farms in drier coastal areas; rooftop solar in Sant Cristòfor
* '''Bagasse/biomass''': Plants linked to sugar cooperatives in Jala and Migjorn provinces
* '''Waste-to-energy''': Municipal waste processing facility in Sant Cristòfor

== Ownership ==

EREB is wholly owned by the [[Government of Balboa]]. It operates under the oversight of the Ministry of Energy and Natural Resources and is not listed on any stock exchange.

